An abundance of bushland, parks and sporting fields now border the length of the river, which is framed by various bike paths and walking trails. At around midday on Thursday, February 5, 2015, two walkers were strolling along the eastern banks of the Maribanong River in Mooney Ponds, a suburb 6km out of Melbourne. They passed the renowned Boathouse Restaurant, owned by celebrity chef Gary Meaghan, which was bustling as usual, as was the Anglers' Tavern, a riverside pub located a little further downstream on the adjacent shore. The Maribanong Park, which flanks the popular eatery, was full of visitors, dog walkers, joggers and children enjoying the warm summer day. At 1.30pm, as the pair continued along the palm and gum tree-lined track, they noticed something floating upstream in the murky waters of the river. It was a white plastic grocery bag with something inside. Upon taking a closer look, they immediately contacted police who promptly arrived to the riverbank. Curious patrons of the nearby establishments watched on as officers stood on a wooden jetty and used a pole to fish the bag from the water.
郁郁葱葱的灌木丛、公园和运动场现在绵延在河流两岸，河岸四周环绕着各种自行车道和步行小径。2015年2月5日星期四中午左右，两名散步者正沿着墨尔本市郊6公里处的穆尼庞兹的马里比尔农河东岸漫步。他们经过了由名厨加里·梅根经营的著名船屋餐厅，这里一如既往地热闹非凡，稍下游相邻岸边的河畔酒吧垂钓者酒馆也是如此。毗邻这家热门餐馆的马里比尔农公园里挤满了游客、遛狗者、慢跑者和享受温暖夏日的儿童。下午1点30分，当两人继续沿着这条两旁种满棕榈树和桉树的小径前行时，他们注意到河水浑浊的水面上有什么东西在逆流而上。这是一个白色的塑料杂货袋，里面装着东西。仔细一看，他们立即联系了警方，警方随即赶到河岸。附近场所的好奇顾客驻足观看，警官们站在一个木制码头上，用一根杆子把袋子从水里捞了上来。

